{{also|Martial}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2019|September|22}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|martial}}, {{m|enm|marcial|marciāl}}, {{m|enm|mercial}}, {{m|enm|mercialle||relating to war, warlike; military; for use in fighting or warfare; brave, hardy; combative, fierce; ruthless, vicious; domineering, overbearing}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=marciāl|pos=''adj.''(1)|noformat=yes|id=MED26889|accessdate=29 March 2019}}</ref> from {{der|en|frm|martial}} (modern {{cog|fr|martial||martial}}), or directly from its {{glossary|etymon}} {{der|en|la|mārtiālis||of or pertaining to Mars, the Roman god of war}}, from {{m|la|Mārtius||of or pertaining to Mars}} + {{m|la|-ālis|pos=''suffix forming adjectives of relationship''}}. The English word is cognate with {{cog|it|marziale||martial}}, {{cog|pt|marcial||martial}}, {{cog|es|marcial||martial}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=adj.'' and ''n|id=114431|date=December 2000|nodot=yes}}; {{R:Oxford Dictionaries Online}}</ref>

